{"name":"Granrojo Jellyfish","hex":"#9e6858","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jellyfish","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An almost transparent aquatic animal; any one of the acalephs, especially one of the larger species, having a jellylike appearance.","Any of various species of cnidarians in the subphylum Medusozoa, including box jellyfish (class Cubozoa), true jellyfish (class Scyphozoa), stalked jellyfish (class Staurozoa), and certain hydrozoans.\n The medusa phase of these animals rather than a younger life stage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jellyfish"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tiburonia","blocks":[{"text":"Tiburonia is a genus of jellyfish in the family Ulmaridae. It was reported in 2003, following the discovery of its only species yet identified, Tiburonia granrojo. It was discovered by a crew from MBARI led by George Matsumoto. Pieces of the medusae were collected for morphological analysis, which eventually led to sequencing and taxonomic identification. The discovery of this organism led to not only a new species, but a new subfamily of Ulmaridae, called Tiburoniinae. Its genus was named Tiburonia after the ROV the crew were using, called Tiburon, meaning \"shark\" in Spanish. Its species name, granrojo, meaning \"big red\" in Spanish, leading to its English-language name, the big red jellyfish."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Tiburonia_granrojo-_noaa_expl0827.jpg/330px-Tiburonia_granrojo-_noaa_expl0827.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiburonia","wikidata":"Q21091020"},{"title":"Jellyfish","blocks":[{"text":"Jellyfish, also known as sea jellies or simply jellies, are the medusa-phase of certain gelatinous members of the subphylum Medusozoa, which is a major part of the phylum Cnidaria. Jellyfish are mainly free-swimming marine animals, although a few are anchored to the seabed by stalks rather than being motile. They are made of an umbrella-shaped main body made of mesoglea, known as the bell, and a collection of trailing tentacles on the underside."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Jelly_cc11.jpg/330px-Jelly_cc11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jellyfish","wikidata":"Q30178"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Tiburonia_granrojo_Unusualjellyfish.jpg/500px-Tiburonia_granrojo_Unusualjellyfish.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Tiburonia_granrojo_Unusualjellyfish.jpg/500px-Tiburonia_granrojo_Unusualjellyfish.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATiburonia_granrojo_Unusualjellyfish.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Tiburonia_granrojo-_noaa_expl0827_detail1.jpg/500px-Tiburonia_granrojo-_noaa_expl0827_detail1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Tiburonia_granrojo-_noaa_expl0827_detail1.jpg/500px-Tiburonia_granrojo-_noaa_expl0827_detail1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATiburonia_granrojo-_noaa_expl0827_detail1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Tiburonia_granrojo-_noaa_expl0783.jpg/500px-Tiburonia_granrojo-_noaa_expl0783.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Tiburonia_granrojo-_noaa_expl0783.jpg/1280px-Tiburonia_granrojo-_noaa_expl0783.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb